1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,217
A bestselling author who can't move or speak.

2
00:00:06,217 --> 00:00:09,791
A house on a lake that has already taken too much.

3
00:00:09,791 --> 00:00:13,294
And a manuscript that was never meant to be read.

4
00:00:13,294 --> 00:00:14,366
This is Verity:

5
00:00:14,366 --> 00:00:19,298
the story, the twist, and the ending everyone is still arguing about.

6
00:00:19,298 --> 00:00:20,728
Full spoilers ahead.

7
00:00:21,828 --> 00:00:27,364
Lowen Ashleigh is a struggling writer, broke and about to lose her apartment.

8
00:00:27,364 --> 00:00:30,528
Then an offer arrives that she can't refuse:

9
00:00:30,528 --> 00:00:41,241
finish the remaining books in a hugely popular thriller series by Verity Crawford, a famous author left unable to move or speak after a car accident.

10
00:00:42,341 --> 00:00:48,914
Verity's husband, Jeremy, invites Lowen to stay at the family home to go through his wife's notes.

11
00:00:48,914 --> 00:00:50,725
It's a house full of grief.

12
00:00:50,725 --> 00:00:59,109
The Crawfords have lost their daughter Harper, and Jeremy is raising their young son, Crew, while caring for Verity upstairs.

13
00:01:00,209 --> 00:01:02,259
Verity is there, always there.

14
00:01:02,259 --> 00:01:02,737
Silent.

15
00:01:02,737 --> 00:01:03,352
Watching.

16
00:01:03,352 --> 00:01:05,607
Or at least, that's how it feels.

17
00:01:06,707 --> 00:01:14,034
Buried in the chaos of Verity's office, Lowen finds something that isn't on any publishing schedule:

18
00:01:14,034 --> 00:01:15,280
an autobiography.

19
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:16,526
It isn't fiction.

20
00:01:16,526 --> 00:01:18,504
It reads like a confession.

21
00:01:19,604 --> 00:01:33,940
Page by page, Verity describes resenting her own children, being jealous of the love Jeremy gives them, and — in the darkest chapters — suggesting that the tragedies that took her family were no accident.

22
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:36,313
Lowen keeps reading.

23
00:01:36,313 --> 00:01:37,841
She knows she shouldn't.

24
00:01:37,841 --> 00:01:42,869
And the more she reads, the less the official story of this family makes sense.

25
00:01:43,969 --> 00:01:46,861
Meanwhile, the house starts to feel wrong.

26
00:01:46,861 --> 00:01:52,232
Lowen becomes convinced she has seen Verity move when no one else was looking.

27
00:01:52,232 --> 00:01:55,468
And at the same time, she's falling for Jeremy.

28
00:01:56,568 --> 00:01:59,725
Eventually, Lowen shows Jeremy the manuscript.

29
00:01:59,725 --> 00:02:04,803
He reads what his wife wrote about their children — and everything breaks.

30
00:02:05,903 --> 00:02:07,249
He confronts Verity.

31
00:02:07,249 --> 00:02:08,865
And the truth comes out:

32
00:02:08,865 --> 00:02:10,211
she has been faking.

33
00:02:10,211 --> 00:02:11,087
She can move.

34
00:02:11,087 --> 00:02:12,029
She can speak.

35
00:02:12,029 --> 00:02:19,300
In the fallout, Jeremy kills her — and with Lowen's help, they stage it to look like an accidental drowning.

36
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:25,090
Seven months later, Lowen and Jeremy are building a life together.

37
00:02:25,090 --> 00:02:28,359
Then Lowen finds something Verity left behind:

38
00:02:28,359 --> 00:02:30,491
a letter, addressed to Jeremy.

39
00:02:31,591 --> 00:02:37,266
In it, Verity says the manuscript was only a writing exercise — a way to process grief.

40
00:02:37,266 --> 00:02:39,679
That she never resented her children.

41
00:02:39,679 --> 00:02:42,027
That Harper's death was an accident.

42
00:02:42,027 --> 00:02:46,137
And that the car crash that injured her was no accident at all:

43
00:02:46,137 --> 00:02:50,246
Jeremy read the manuscript, believed it, and tried to kill her.

44
00:02:50,246 --> 00:02:56,508
She pretended to be helpless because she was afraid, waiting for the chance to escape with Crew.

45
00:02:57,608 --> 00:02:59,120
So which Verity is real?

46
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:01,514
The manuscript says she was a monster.

47
00:03:01,514 --> 00:03:06,239
The letter says she was a grieving mother trapped with a dangerous husband.

48
00:03:06,239 --> 00:03:12,412
Both were written by a woman who made her living telling stories from the villain's point of view.

49
00:03:12,412 --> 00:03:17,704
The film never hands us the answer — and Lowen keeps the letter's secret to herself.

50
00:03:18,804 --> 00:03:22,768
If you've read Colleen Hoover's novel, you'll spot changes.

51
00:03:22,768 --> 00:03:25,992
Most of the book's sleepwalking subplot is gone.

52
00:03:25,992 --> 00:03:33,852
The tension between Lowen and Verity gets a charge it never had on the page, including a kiss that isn't in the book.

53
00:03:33,852 --> 00:03:42,249
And director Michael Showalter has said several endings were filmed, with test audiences pushing to keep the ambiguity alive.

54
00:03:43,349 --> 00:03:46,757
In the end, Verity isn't really about who is lying.

55
00:03:46,757 --> 00:03:49,297
It's about who gets to tell the story.

56
00:03:49,297 --> 00:03:51,569
So — the manuscript or the letter?

57
00:03:51,569 --> 00:03:56,981
Tell me which one you believe in the comments, and subscribe for more breakdowns.
